==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|repreven}}, {{m|enm|reproven}}, from {{der|en|xno|reprover}}, {{der|en|frm|reprouver}}, from {{der|en|la|reprobāre}}. {{doublet|en|reprobate}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈpɹuːv/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-reprove.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːv|s=2}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To express [[disapproval]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}} {{rfex|en}}<!--need INTRANSITIVE examples-->
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:criticize}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[criticise]], [[rebuke]], or [[reprimand]] (someone), usually in a gentle and kind tone. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Proverbs|9|8|passage='''Reprove''' not a scorner, lest he hate thee: rebuke a wise man, and he will love thee.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[deny]] or [[reject]] (as a feeling, behaviour, action, ''etc.''). {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* '''1982''', {{w|Lawrence Durrell}}, ''Constance'', Faber & Faber 2004 {{qualifier|Avignon Quintet}}, p. 856:

